00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:03.429 align:start position:0%
the cdc eviction moratorium comes to an  end this  weekend leaving a lot of people here in  las vegas in danger of losing their  homes  but there are options if you're still  unable to make ends meet  sasha loftis spoke with a local expert  who has advice for anyone who's scared  and struggling sasha  denise so many people here in las vegas  don't know what they're going to do is  that eviction moratorium comes to an end  this weekend  i spoke with one man tonight who hit  some tough times but made it to the  other side  he and a local expert i spoke with said  it's important to reach out and ask for  advice when you need it  for eugene pena this last year has been  tough  bills came up things started coming up  you know and it was just one of those  things where  you know it kind of snowballed he was  laid off last march  and described the stress his family felt  when they could no longer  make their mortgage payments there was a  lot of uncertainty  we didn't know what tomorrow was going  to bring we didn't know what the next  few months were going to bring  pena is back on the right track now but  he knows  so many others who fear for the future  as the cdc  eviction moratorium comes to an end  sunday  there are options so you can help with  it there's ways to get out of it pif  lending managing broker andrew levitz  says to reach out to your landlord or  lender  to discuss your options if you're  renting from someone there's a good  chance you have a real person on the  other side of that transaction  just talk to them there's also rental  assistance available  and as long as you apply new nevada  legislation  puts more time on the table as long as  you're trying and making the attempt to  make the payment and whatever it means  possible  governor select did sign a bill to go  ahead and put in the action you can't  get evicted  eugene says no matter your situation  chances are  others have been there before so there's  no shame  in asking for help no person is an  island you know there are resources out  there  leavitt says homeowners also have  options to extend a pause  on payments or apply for a different  type of loan modification  now landlords they have options too they  can apply for a small business loan  or help through that new nevada  legislation that was announced this week  we have more information and more  application information on our website  8newsnow.com reporting live in las vegas  tonight sasha loftis  8 news now
 

